FILE design/RimMandrake/hugethings_codex_2026-11-09/QUESTIONS.md
1: Historical question bank: all eight drafts were answered in ANSWERS_OWNER.md. Draft 03 describes the rejected access-open rule; it is not a current requirement.
2: 
3: DRY RUN — HugeThings, proposed root and question bank. All wording and guesses below are mine, not Mandrake's words. Nothing is approved or sent.
4: 
5: This mod bundles several built promises: huge plants, huge animals, titan movement and damage, and the aftermath of a titan's death. Each needs its own proof.
6: 
7: PROPOSED ROOT
8: 
9: HugeThings makes huge plants and animals behave like the size you see: mass has consequences. A giant fungus has a solid stem and roots, open space beneath its cap, and a picture you can actually click; growing trunks respect the colony already around them. A huge animal has a body you can select, and a titan meets gaps, walls, roofs and trenches as a creature of its breadth: squeezing where it fits, breaking what it can, and being held by what really stops it. Its passage can wreck the colony simply because something that large walked through. A biome's own giants announce their approach with tremors. The largest dead titan becomes a landmark worked over days, with ordinary meat's rules for rot, while scavengers and visiting people compete for what remains. These are several connected promises, each owed its own demonstration. HugeThings supplies the common machinery; the biomes supply the living things, Large Pawns supplies their larger occupied bodies when loaded, and the plant and animal settings let the player tune their consequences.
10: 
11: My claim: making the visible size true is the common idea that joins these features.
12: The strongest objection: that paragraph can sound as if every part already works. Some parts are built but unaccepted; normal corpse rot and a real dispute over remains still have gaps. This is a proposed promise, not a report of passing tests.
13: 
14: These are eight drafts to choose from, not an eight-question interrogation. I would begin with 01–02, then serve at most three at a time as your answers make the later ones useful. A short phrase, a different scene, or “this doesn't matter to me” is enough. No reply is being assumed. I recommend the crossing-and-aftermath scene first because you already explained how its two actions belong together.
